---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import DefinitionBox from '../components/content/DefinitionBox.astro';
import Callout from '../components/content/Callout.astro';
import CompareTable from '../components/content/CompareTable.astro';
import FundamentalTriad from '../components/diagrams/FundamentalTriad.astro';
import AmdahlBar from '../components/diagrams/AmdahlBar.astro';

const comparisonRows = [
  {
    left: 'Human community remains the primary trust mechanism',
    right: 'Agent ecosystem operates on corpus quality, not relationship',
  },
  {
    left: 'Ambassadors earn trust through presence and authentic voice',
    right: 'Agent Champions earn relevance through validated configurations',
  },
  {
    left: 'Documentation written for human navigation',
    right: 'Documentation must also serve agent retrieval with semantic precision',
  },
  {
    left: 'SDK designed for developer ergonomics',
    right: 'SDK also needs agent-parseable error models and structured schemas',
  },
  {
    left: 'Metrics: reach, engagement, community health',
    right: 'Metrics: FAISR, Amdahl ceiling, recipe freshness, MCP adoption tier',
  },
  {
    left: 'Human community patterns grow more valuable as AI proliferates',
    right: 'Agent ecosystem layer is where AI-assisted development wins or loses',
  },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="What Is Agentic Developer Relations?"
  description="The definition of Agentic Developer Relations — the discipline within Developer Relations responsible for the AI agent layer of every developer platform."
>
  <section class="container-site pt-16 pb-4">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Definition</p>
    <h1 class="max-w-3xl">What is Agentic Developer Relations?</h1>
    <p class="lead text-muted mt-5 max-w-2xl">
      The definition, the three principles, the primary metric,
      and the way it extends the Fundamental Triad.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container-prose pb-16">
    <DefinitionBox term="Agentic Developer Relations (AR)">
      The discipline within Developer Relations responsible for ensuring that
      AI coding agents and autonomous AI systems can successfully integrate
      with, consume, and represent a platform accurately — through deliberate
      cultivation of agent-consumable documentation, validated prompt recipes,
      machine-readable schemas, and the ongoing measurement of agent
      ecosystem health.
    </DefinitionBox>

    <p class="mt-6">
      Agentic Developer Relations does not replace Developer Relations. It is the next
      structural layer of the discipline — required because the audience for
      every developer platform now includes a non-human member: the AI coding
      agent that an increasing fraction of developers use to generate
      integration code before they write a single line themselves.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>The core insight: two audiences, one platform</h2>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Every developer platform has always had one audience: human developers.
        Documentation, SDKs, APIs, tutorials, support channels — all of it
        designed for people who read, type, ask questions, and remember.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        That has changed. When a developer asks GitHub Copilot, Cursor, or
        Claude Code to write an integration with your platform, there are now
        two parties making decisions: the developer, who evaluates the output,
        and the AI agent, who generates it. The agent doesn't navigate your
        documentation. It retrieves specific sections as context for
        generation tasks. It doesn't tolerate ambiguity. It amplifies every
        imprecision in your API surface and every gap in your documentation
        into integration failures at scale.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        The developer experience of your platform is now partly determined by
        how well AI agents can work with it. DevRel has always been
        responsible for the developer experience. Agentic Developer Relations is the
        recognition that this responsibility now extends to the non-human
        member of the development partnership.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-14">Where this sits: Agent Experience</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        The idea that agents are users of a platform has a name that
        predates this site. Mathias Biilmann of Netlify introduced
        <a href="https://www.netlify.com/agent-experience/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agent Experience (AX)</a>
        in January 2025: the experience AI agents have as users of a
        product or platform.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        AX is a property of a platform, as Developer Experience is.
        Developer Relations is one of the practices that improves Developer
        Experience. Agentic Developer Relations is the corresponding
        practice for Agent Experience: the part of DevRel that finds out
        how well agents fare on a platform and carries that back to the
        product, the documentation, and the community.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-primary-surface">
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold"></th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Property of the platform</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Practice that works on it</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Humans</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Developer Experience (DX)</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Developer Relations</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Agents</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Agent Experience (AX)</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Agentic Developer Relations</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Engineering teams own much of what determines AX: API design, auth,
        error models. Agentic Developer Relations does not replace that
        work. It brings the evidence of what developers and their agents
        are trying to do, and where they fail.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>The three principles</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      <div class="border-t-4 border-primary border-x border-b border-border rounded-lg bg-surface p-6">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-2">Principle 01</p>
        <h3 class="text-heading text-lg font-bold mb-3 leading-snug">
          Shaped by deliberate choices or by neglect.
        </h3>
        <p class="text-body text-sm leading-relaxed">
          When an AI tool is pointed at your platform, it draws on whatever is
          in its context: public docs, GitHub code, Stack Overflow, MCP server
          definitions. That corpus is being shaped right now. The only
          question is whether it is being shaped intentionally.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-t-4 border-amber border-x border-b border-border rounded-lg bg-surface p-6">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-2 text-amber">Principle 02</p>
        <h3 class="text-heading text-lg font-bold mb-3 leading-snug">
          Agent failures are silent and systemic.
        </h3>
        <p class="text-body text-sm leading-relaxed">
          A developer whose AI tool generates a broken integration may debug
          silently, find a workaround, or abandon the platform. They are
          unlikely to file a support ticket. The failure is invisible. The
          traditional DevRel dashboard shows green.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-t-4 border-purple border-x border-b border-border rounded-lg bg-surface p-6">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-2 text-purple">Principle 03</p>
        <h3 class="text-heading text-lg font-bold mb-3 leading-snug">
          Encoded judgment is the moat.
        </h3>
        <p class="text-body text-sm leading-relaxed">
          AI models are commodities. What is not commoditized is encoded
          judgment — which error states need handling, which architectural
          patterns hold under load, which edge cases the docs miss. That
          judgment, encoded into machine-accessible artifacts, compounds.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-site">
      <div class="container-prose px-0 mb-6">
        <h2>The Amdahl Ceiling</h2>
        <p class="mt-5">
          At NVIDIA GTC in March 2026, Jeff Dean observed that once agents
          work far faster than people, the start-up time of their tools
          becomes "an Amdahl's Law-like bottleneck": make the model
          infinitely fast and you get "a factor of two or three" reduction
          in latency, if tools are a significant fraction of the work
          (<a href="https://officechai.com/ai/will-need-to-re-engineer-tools-so-that-they-can-keep-up-with-the-speeds-of-ai-agents-googles-jeff-dean/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">as reported by OfficeChai</a>). The tools, APIs, auth
          flows, and documentation the model touches — all designed for
          human hands — are that fraction.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5">
          This is Amdahl's Law applied to platform integrations. If 80% of the
          time an AI agent spends integrating with your platform is consumed
          by human-speed tool interactions, then no model, however capable,
          can make the whole integration more than 1.25× faster. Removing
          that overhead is worth up to 5×. A
          competitor who has rebuilt their integration surface for
          agent-native consumption has a structurally higher ceiling.
        </p>
      </div>
      <AmdahlBar />
      <div class="container-prose px-0 mt-6">
        <p>
          DevRel's role in raising that ceiling: identify where it's binding,
          make it visible to the platform engineering team, and build the
          interim artifacts — structured schemas, semantic documentation,
          validated recipes — that lower the agent tax while deeper platform
          investment follows.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          <a href="/the-amdahl-tax/" class="font-semibold">
            Read the full Amdahl Tax argument →
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>How it extends the Fundamental Triad</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        The Fundamental Triad of Developer Relations — Community, Company,
        Customer — assumed every vertex was human. In the Agentic Developer Relations
        era, each vertex gains an extension into the agent ecosystem.
      </p>
    </div>
    <FundamentalTriad />
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>The primary metric: First-Attempt Integration Success Rate</h2>

      <p class="mt-5">
        Traditional DevRel has always struggled to produce metrics that
        connect directly to adoption. Agentic Developer Relations offers one
        that is at least directly measurable.
      </p>

      <Callout type="primary" label="FAISR">
        <strong>First-Attempt Integration Success Rate:</strong> Of all
        integrations generated by AI coding tools using a platform's published
        materials as context, what percentage produce working code without
        manual correction on the first attempt?
      </Callout>

      <p class="mt-5">
        This metric is measurable through controlled testing. It is
        approximable through API log analysis. It is validatable through
        developer surveys. The working hypothesis is that it connects to
        adoption: developers whose AI-assisted integrations succeed are
        more likely to adopt a platform, and developers who meet systematic
        failures are more likely to choose an alternative. That link is
        plausible. It has not yet been demonstrated.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Most DevRel programs today cannot answer the FAISR question for their
        platform. Agentic Developer Relations exists to change that.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        <a href="/measurement/" class="font-semibold">
          How to instrument FAISR for your platform →
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0 mb-4">
      <h2>What changes, what stays the same</h2>
    </div>
    <CompareTable
      leftHeading="Traditional DevRel — unchanged"
      rightHeading="Agentic Developer Relations — new layer required"
      rows={comparisonRows}
    />

    <div class="container-prose px-0 mt-8">
      <p>
        <a href="/devrel-patterns-reframed/" class="font-semibold">
          Continue to: The 37 Patterns Reframed →
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
